CDX cooperates with the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia and Kandal Provincial Administration to share knowledge on derivatives trading
(Kandal): The Cambodian Derivatives Exchange (CDX) in collaboration with the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia and the Kandal Provincial Administration has organized a workshop in Kandal province, which is the seventh goal of the 2019 provincial workshop.
The workshop was organized under the theme "What is Derivatives Trading: Investment Preparation and Relevant Awareness?" The purpose of this workshop is to share knowledge, skills, experience, regulations and investment opportunities to the public throughout Cambodia.
Derivatives refer to contracts for price gaps on the original financial instruments derived from equity securities, bonds, precious metals, crude oil, natural gas, agricultural products, currencies, as well as hedging plans, and which are traded in the market. These derivatives are financial instruments that are a type of securities.
The workshop was organized by Mr. Sok Dara, Deputy Director General of the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia and Mr. Nou Peng Chandara, Deputy Governor of Kandal Province.
Mr. Nou Peng Chandara highlighted the potential of Kandal Province and expressed the Royal Government of Cambodia's interest in the development of all sectors, especially the economic sector, which is evident through the economic reforms that help Cambodia to Economic growth is around 7% per year.
Through this workshop, Mr. Nou Peng Chandara also expects the participants to gain more financial knowledge, especially in the derivatives market.
Mr. Sok Dara presented the current situation of the derivatives market in Cambodia as well as the position of the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia in expanding the derivatives market further development. He encouraged investors to increase their knowledge and be ready to invest in derivatives to contribute to national economic development.
"The derivatives market has greatly benefited the Cambodian economy by creating job opportunities for university graduates, creating investment income opportunities, and shifting from savings to savings," he said. "Investment, creating new investment options, diverting foreign investment into the country and increasing revenue for the state through taxes."
He said that to date, CDX and the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia have cooperated with the provincial administration to organize workshops in 12 provinces, including: Sihanoukville, Kampong Cham, Siem Reap, Banteay Meanchey and Battambang. Takeo, Kampot, Kep, Kampong Chhnang, Pursat, Pailin and Kandal provinces.
