Health Minister: By 2019, Cambodia will reduce the prevalence of blindness to 0.37%, more than 60,000 Cambodians are blind
(Phnom Penh): Professor Mam Bunheng, Minister of Health, said that Cambodia has reduced the prevalence of blindness from 0.38% in 2007 to 0.37% in 2019, which has helped improve health. The eyes of the people have become even better.
The decrease in the prevalence of blindness is due to the fact that people are more aware of the importance of eye health and have adequate eye care services from the local to the national level, which makes it easier for people to visit and Treat surgery when there is an eye problem.
Statement by the Minister at the National Workshop on Dissemination of the Results of the Rapid Survey on Avoidable Blindness in Cambodia and the Review of the Strategic Plan for the Management and Prevention of Blindness 2016-2020 on 30 May This October at the Phnom Penh Hotel.
Prof. Mam Bunheng hopes that the data from this research will be used as an important aid to eye health service providers and action planners to address blindness and visual impairment in Cambodia, both nationally and sub-nationally. Further decreasing further.
He urged the National Eye Health Program to continue to work with partner organizations to train more ophthalmologists and infrastructure to facilitate people's access to eye care services.
Mr. Kong Neath, Director of Bright In Holding Vision Rehabilitation Institute, vowed to continue to support the Ministry of Health in training human resources in ophthalmology and educating the community on eye health to help reduce blindness and vision loss in Cambodia. But further reduced.
He added that at present, we have 70 ophthalmologists, 237 ophthalmologists, 118 optometrists working in 21 public institutions of provincial hospitals and Phnom Penh.
He added that in order to facilitate eye care for the poor in remote provinces, the National Eye Health Program has set up a mobile team of doctors to examine and treat cataracts twice a month. In addition, we have produced important eye health education materials in Cambodia that have been broadcast on radio, television and Facebook.
However, Professor Ngy Meng stated that through the research results, we found that the main reasons that prevent patients from receiving eye care services include fear of cataract surgery. About 45 percent.
About 17% of patients think that there is no need for eye treatment because they have limited vision and can walk. 11.8 percent.
