Mr. Em Chanmakara wants stakeholders to increase the ability to produce documents or books for people with disabilities
He added that people with disabilities who have difficulty reading printed documents can not get all the information from print media as usual because people with this type of disability can not see or read the text, which means they need documents in Convenient formats, such as plain text or voice aids, e-books, or large print documents.
The remarks made by the Secretary General of the General Secretariat of the Council for the Action of Persons with Disabilities were made during the workshop "Input Collection Consultation to Reach the Marxist Treaty" on October 28, 2019 at the Phnom Penh Hotel. With the participation of Mr. Vong Soth, Minister of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation and Chairman of the Disability Action Council, officials from relevant ministries and institutions and many NGOs.
The workshop, organized by the General Secretariat of the Council for the Action of Persons with Disabilities in collaboration with the United Nations Development Program (UNDP), seeks to collect input into the ratification of the Marrakesh Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities. "The Treaty of Marquez."
The treaty enables people with disabilities to effectively access basic human rights knowledge in education, the labor market, and cultural activities. This treaty is especially important for the blind and visually impaired, who make up a high percentage of the total number of people with disabilities in Cambodia, at 35 percent.
Em Chanmakara added that millions of blind or visually impaired people around the world are missing out on significant development opportunities and benefits due to a lack of easy-to-read documents or text. It is estimated that less than 1% of developing countries print books in less than 7% of developed textbooks.
He added that the problem of the blind or visually impaired in Cambodia today is not different from the problem of the blind in other countries in the world, such as: Lack of printed documents for study, research documents in the library, office. In court, dharma books or dharma books.
In order to comply with the Marquez Treaty and take full advantage of it, the Royal Government of Cambodia should consider the possibility of amending the Copyright Law in order to facilitate and provide exceptions to allow the printing and distribution of manuscripts in a convenient format. Easy to read and understand and can be widely distributed for people with disabilities both inside and outside the country.
Under the highly responsible leadership of Samdech Akka Moha Sena Padei Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia and Honorary Chairman of the Council for Action for the Disabled, Blind and Visually Impaired, we are fortunate and fortunate to be educated and Vocational training, which is the foundation of promoting the rights of people with disabilities.
It should be noted that the Royal Government of Cambodia has established the National Institute of Special Education, which is the staff of the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports on the training and training of teachers, students with disabilities and other duties to serve the interests of people with disabilities, especially the blind. Deaf and so on.
