(Kratie): Mekong River Dolphin Day 2019 has been taking place from October 25-27 under the theme "Increase dolphins, increase tourism, increase livelihood" with a joint commitment to conserve dolphins in Cambodia.
The opening ceremony of Mekong River Dolphin Day 2019 was held last evening under the presidency of Mr. Top Sopheak, Undersecretary of State of the Ministry of Tourism in Kratie Province.
On that occasion, he highly appreciated the Kratie Provincial Administration, the Department of Tourism, Departments around the province and WWF, which co-organized the Mekong River Dolphin Day 2019 by disseminating to people better understand the importance of biology and Conservation of Mekong River dolphins to contribute to socio-economic development aimed at maintaining the biological and ecological balance along the Mekong River and improving the livelihoods of local people.
The Undersecretary of State pointed out that dolphins are one of the 58 endangered fisheries species identified in the Royal Government's sub-decree and are also on the Union Red List. International for the Conservation of Natural Resources (IUCN) is an endangered species and a living national treasure of Cambodia.
Currently, there are only 92 dolphins in Cambodia and they are living along the Mekong River in Kratie and Stung Treng provinces, which requires careful conservation with a clear conservation strategy.
Mr. Seng Teak, WWF Country Director, stated that through WWF's activities in Cambodia, the organization has been working closely with the Fisheries Administration and the Kratie and Stung Treng Fisheries Districts and Stung Treng Provinces to protect and conserve the Mekong River dolphins as a result. Proudly, we have seen an increase in the number of 20 new dolphins between 2018 and 2019, with six dead dolphins.
In addition, the organization has focused on the project to change people's livelihoods from illegal fishing around the dolphin sanctuary, in collaboration with the Department of Tourism, to establish an ecotourism community by providing employment opportunities to local people through the provision of services. Accommodation, transportation services, catering services, community guides and other fitness tourism services.
Please be informed that the opening ceremony of Mekong River Dolphin Day was held with the participation of all stakeholders, local people and about 1,000 tourists, organizing trade fairs, songs, performances, greeting dances and car dances. And the program to eat grapefruit, kralan and neem, which are local products.
