Mr. Neth Pheaktra calls on people to plant trees on campus or pagoda and in the community for a clean and green Cambodia
(Prey Veng): Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, called on the people to participate in the implementation of environmental friendliness in daily life, loving the environment and natural resources more, especially to plant trees in the school or pagoda and in the community. Its for a clean and green Cambodia.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra's call was made during a tree planting ceremony under the theme "One Tree, One Life" organized by Youth Star Cambodia in collaboration with Brunei Donsol Insurance Company in Kampong Prasat Commune, Peam Chor District, Prey Veng Province this morning. October 26, 2019.
He stressed the importance of natural resources for the environment and human and animal life, and planting trees on campus or pagoda helps to create greenery, create a comfortable environment and make it a comfortable place for students to learn.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment said that the planting of trees creates greenery in accordance with the principles of the Environment Friendly School, which the Ministry of Environment has been cooperating to encourage schools across the country to practice good environmental friendliness as a place to cultivate human resources. Quality and environmentally responsible.
Mr. Pheaktra also said that planting trees in schools or pagodas is a practical measure in adapting to climate change and global warming. Climate change is adversely affecting the country's development process and is a global problem. Cambodia is not a country that causes climate change, but one that suffers from climate change.
He emphasized that the planting of luxury trees such as Thnong, Beng, Neang, Nuon, Koki, etc. in the community of pagodas and in schools is a contribution to the protection and conservation of biodiversity, especially some rare trees in the district, adding natural resources. Cambodia's existing forests are managed and conserved.
According to the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, the Ministry of Environment is currently managing and conserving protected areas, including national parks, wildlife sanctuaries, landscape areas, multi-use areas, Ramsar sites and natural heritage sites, and biodiversity conservation corridors. More than 60 nature reserves with a total area of more than 7.2 million hectares, equivalent to about 41% of the land area of Cambodia.
The Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment also said that the Ministry of Environment has asked the Cambodian people to participate in protecting and conserving the natural resources that Cambodia has today for future generations.
