Mekong Dolphin Conservation Aimed at Sustainable Tourism Development
The purpose of this workshop is to inspire and share ideas that are important to all participants about the importance of conserving the Upper Mekong Dolphin, a rare mammal of the whole nation. Linking and the role of dolphins in contributing to social development in areas such as tourism, agriculture, fisheries and the economy.
On the other hand, participate in defining some perspectives and strategic plans for dolphin conservation in the development of sustainable tourism in the region.
Dolphins are a rare mammal that lives along the upper Mekong River between Kratie and Stung Treng provinces in Cambodia. According to the latest monitoring and recording by the Statistical Unit on the number of breeding dolphins present in the Mekong River, there are more than 92. Dolphins are considered an important natural resource in attracting tourists to visit Kratie province.
