This growth was supported by three main sectors:
* First, industrial growth is projected to slow to 8.9 percent in 2020 from 10.7 percent in 2019 due to slower growth of the sub-sector and slightly slower growth. Of the construction sub-sector, although the non-garment industry sub-sector, both for export and domestic market, may achieve slightly better growth than in 2019.
The garment sub-sector is projected to grow at a slower pace due to the slowdown in global economic growth and anticipation of the possible impact of uncertainties on trade preferences with some partners. At the same time, according to recent trends, the construction sub-sector may face market adjustments that will slow down the growth of the sector.
However, non-garment industries such as food and beverage, automotive assembly, electronics, parts, furniture and cement, both for export and domestic demand, will have high growth momentum in terms of investment expansion. Existing and both in terms of new investments.
The wholesale-retail sub-sector maintained good growth, supported by the growth of domestic consumption reflected in the increase in per capita income. The real estate sub-sector, meanwhile, is projected to grow at a slower pace due to expectations of a slowdown in demand for high-rise buildings.
In particular, the fisheries sub-sector is expected to grow slightly beyond 2019, driven by the continued growth of the aquaculture sub-sector in line with the growing trend of private investment and the participation of development partners. The livestock sub-sector is projected to grow slightly more than last year due to the expansion of commercial pig and chicken production in the form of contracts between medium farmers and private companies.
In summary, based on the macroeconomic indicators set out above, we can say that Cambodia can continue to achieve high economic growth in the short and medium term. However, the Cambodian economy is still facing a number of risks and challenges, both internal and external, that could jeopardize the sustainability of economic growth and disrupt its development.
In this regard, the Royal Government has decided to set out and continue to implement a number of sharp policy measures in a timely, effective and efficient manner to overcome and address all challenges and risks to ensure macro stability. The economy will achieve high and sustainable economic growth.
It should be noted that in 2020, the Royal Government of Cambodia plans to spend a total of 33,339,633 million riels, equivalent to 8,223 million US dollars for state management. The project expenditure is about 28.01 percent of GDP, an increase of 22.7 percent compared to 2019.
