The Royal Government of Cambodia on October 25, 2019 approved the draft law on finance for the year 2020. According to the draft law, the loan will enable the Royal Government to sign loans from friendly countries and development partners to implement 29 of the 32 major projects.
The projects to be signed for the loan include: 3 World Bank projects, 7 Asian Development Bank projects, 1 German Development Bank project, 8 projects in the People's Republic of China, 2 projects in Japan, and 3 Republic of Korea projects. 3 projects, 3 projects in the French Republic, 1 project in the Kingdom of Thailand and 1 project in the Republic of India.
The draft law also states that the decision to borrow nearly $ 2 billion in foreign currency is based on five key aspects:
1: Growing demand for investment in infrastructure and public services, both in size, quantity and quality to boost the economic recovery in the new phase and in the context of uncertainty.
2. The development project that will sign the concession loan agreement in 2020 is a public investment project in the priority areas (physical infrastructure, economic productivity increase and production productivity) and which has been moving from previous years, but has a strategic character to support Sustainability of medium and long term economic growth
3: All loans with high concession level
4. The current and medium- and long-term public debt situation in Cambodia remains sustainable and the risk is low.
According to the newly approved draft law, as of the end of the first half of 2019, Cambodia owed a total of 7.264 million US dollars in foreign debt. Of that, loans from China accounted for nearly 50 percent.
Cambodians often express concern about foreign loans, fearing they could become insolvent. The draft law states that the size of the loan does not create a debt burden on Cambodia, because as the economy grows, Cambodia's repayment capacity will be higher.
