(Phnom Penh): Lt. Gen. Dy Vichea, Deputy Commissioner General of the National Police and Director of the Central Security Department, on the afternoon of October 25, 2019, closed a basic training workshop on financial investigation and anti-money laundering.
The above training workshop was attended by 37 participants from the judiciary, the Anti-Corruption Unit, the General Commission of the National Police, the General Department of Customs and the Governor of Cambodia, the General Department of Taxation, the Financial Intelligence Unit of Cambodia and the National Commission for Crime Prevention and Suppression. Forest.
The five-day workshop was co-organized by the National Police and the United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime (UNODC) with the aim of building the capacity and technical capacity of law enforcement agencies and competent investigators. On the operation to investigate, prosecute and punish money laundering offenses.
Taking this opportunity, the Lt. Gen. also reminded about the cooperation between the National Police of Cambodia and UNODC, especially on the technical assistance projects provided to the National Police in the past.
Notably, in 2012-2013, UNODC provided technical training programs to investigate money laundering to more than 100 Cambodian police forces (including officials from the judiciary and the Cambodian Financial Intelligence Unit).
Lt. Gen. Di Vichea added that UNODC has continued to increase the level of training of trainers, and as a result, 14 police officers have been assessed and selected as trainers for the technical training program to investigate money laundering.
At the same time, Lt. Gen. Di Vichea also requested the trainees who completed the course today to continue to attend the advanced training seminar on financial investigation and anti-money laundering, which will take place in early 2020. Chinese Foreign Ministry: Britain has not yet confirmed that the 39 bodies in the container were Chinese.
