(Phnom Penh): Cambodia has asked the United States to amend an agreement on the repatriation of citizens who have committed illegal crimes in the United States. The Cambodian side's request now is to see Cambodians who have been deported or deported for their crimes have the opportunity to reunite and visit their relatives in both Cambodia and the United States.
Kuy Kuong, a spokesman for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, told reporters after the meeting that Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon and Patrick Murphy had discussed cooperation between Cambodia and the United States. He said the Cambodian diplomat had asked the United States to amend the repatriation agreement to allow deported Cambodians to reunite with their families.
"The Deputy Prime Minister requested that the MoU be amended," Kuy Kuong said. "So anyone deported for illegal activities may be allowed to visit their families in the United States."
"We focus on humanitarian principles and we want to allow deportees to Cambodia the opportunity to meet and visit their relatives in the United States," a State Department spokesman said.
The US Department of Immigration and Customs confirmed at the time that the number of Cambodians deported increased by 279 percent in 2018 compared to 2017. But did not specify what percentage in 2017.
The department also confirmed that about 1,900 Cambodians with deportation warrants were living in the United States. Of that number, 1,400 are already convicted criminals.
