A young man from Kampong Chhnang province struggles to become a deputy director of the Animal Breeding Laboratory and brings local breeding DNA to study and analyze genetics at the UN
This young agricultural expert named Phim Meng Hak is the son of a farmer in Chronok commune, Kampong Leng district, Kampong Chhnang province.
In a fatherless family with only three dependent siblings, Phim Menghak is one of the hardest-working alumni. Both knowledge and talent gave him the opportunity to learn and be extremely successful.
He is a former Bachelor of Science in Animal Science from the Royal University of Agriculture, Phnom Penh. Due to his academic ability, Mr. Phim Meng Hak also pursued a Master's Degree in Genetics at Khon Khaen University of the Kingdom of Thailand.
Currently, Mr. Phim Menghak is the Deputy Director of the Genetics Laboratory of the Institute of Animal Health and Animal Production Research at the General Department of Animal Health and Animal Production. Phim Meng Hak's prestige caught the attention of Fresh News and he was interviewed while taking DNA from local breeds in Austria.
Mr. Phim Menghak stated that he loves animal husbandry and as a child of farmers, he is more and more attached to agricultural work, especially animal husbandry. He also stated why he was trying to research the agricultural sector, saying that he wanted to see the agricultural sector in Cambodia grow as fast as the main domestic sectors.
"I really want to see the agricultural sector grow rapidly, especially the livestock sub-sector, where its producers can meet all the needs of the market, get high returns and have their own products that can compete with other countries." This is the confirmation of Phim Meng Hak.
The study, published in the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) in Seibersdorf, Austria, was conducted in the National Institute of Animal Health and Production. Animals of the General Department of Animal Health and Animal Production respond to local cattle breeding development policies of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries.
After explaining the genetic analysis, Mr. Phim Meng Hak recalled his bachelor's degree at the Royal University of Agriculture in Phnom Penh. He says he has experienced a lot of difficulties, even the foreign language is limited, and even more difficult is the lack of funds.
"I lack the advisers, especially on the budget," he said. I came to my first year, I ate only factory food, saved money, learned English and paid for the room. Until the second year, I got some part-time work and the teacher allowed me to stay at the university just a little bit.
After going through many hardships, Mr. Phim Meng seems to be a person at a much better level. He now speaks four languages: English, Thai, French and Khmer, and has been a speaker and trained in many countries in Asia and Europe.
He emphasized that in addition to his role as a civil servant, he worked as a part-time teacher at universities, institutes and as a consultant on animal husbandry and rural development projects. He used to be a volunteer and leader of the youth club of our hometown association and volunteer in various youth groups.
In addition, due to his high level of English proficiency, he has also worked as an English teacher, English translator and national and international seminar coordinator. This kind of history makes Mr. Phim Meng Hak evolve from day to day, causing himself to become a good vine for the successor of the nation.
Mr. Phim Menghak gave the idea that there is nothing that we humans can not do as long as there is perseverance. He also instructed the younger generation to have self-confidence to participate in doing something good and to achieve the interests of our country.
At the beginning of the meeting, the President of Indonesia thanked the Prime Minister for attending the swearing-in ceremony. He also thanked Samdech Techo Hun Sen for promoting strong bilateral cooperation between Indonesia and Cambodia.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen expressed hope that in the new term, the President will find a suitable time to visit Cambodia to strengthen relations and cooperation between the two countries, while four former Indonesian presidents have already visited Cambodia.
In this meeting, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also raised the issue of Timor-Leste wanting to become a member of ASEAN. Samdech Techo Hun Sen expressed hope that all ASEAN member countries will decide on East Timor's ASEAN membership at the ASEAN Summit in Bangkok later this year.
Samdech Techo stated that East Timor has made every effort to become an ASEAN member, especially by opening embassies in all 10 ASEAN member countries. Samdech Techo hopes that the President of Indonesia will support Timor-Leste as a member of ASEAN.
The President of Indonesia supports the statement made by Samdech Techo Hun Sen to allow Timor-Leste to become the 11th member of ASEAN and Indonesia supports Timor-Leste. The president said it was a good thing for Timor-Leste to become a member of ASEAN.
As for bilateral relations, the president has seen good progress. Like the health sector, the two countries have joint investment and the transport sector, the two countries have direct flights together.
But the president sees many other potential areas that need to be addressed, such as rail and water transport.
