Sofia is one of Bulgaria's major economic centers and has attracted international business in areas such as financial services, electronics, information technology and telecommunications. Although the city has a population of 16% of Bulgaria's total population, Sofia has played an important role in the development of the country, where it contributes 34% of gross domestic product (GDP).
Sofia has become a popular tourist destination, with 65% of foreign visitors coming mostly from Italy, Germany, Greece and the UK. The growing number of tourists is expected to bring positive points to the tourism sector, including the modernization and development of services.
Please note that today is the last day of the official visit of Samdech Techo Hun Sen and the delegation in Bulgaria. Samdech Techo Hun Sen and his delegation will leave Sofia this afternoon and will arrive in Cambodia this morning, October 18, with great results for the nation and its people. Cambodia.
Recall that the Kingdom of Cambodia and the Republic of Bulgaria established diplomatic relations on October 9, 1967, but were severed in 1975 by war and genocide.
In 1979, Cambodia and Bulgaria re-established diplomatic relations. On August 10, 1999, the Royal Government of Cambodia decided to close its embassy in Sofia due to lack of funds.
In the field of education cooperation from 1980 to 1989, the Republic of Bulgaria provided scholarships to 159 Cambodian students to study in Bulgaria.
