Minister Ith Sam Heng: Violence and harassment in the workplace are barriers to labor productivity and workers' well-being
(Phnom Penh): At the Regional Workshop on Women's Workplace at the Le Royal Hotel on the morning of October 17, 2019, Mr. Ith Sam Heng, Minister of Labor and Vocational Training, stated that violence and harassment in the workplace It is a barrier to the productivity and well-being of workers.
The Minister continued that to solve this problem, the Royal Government of Cambodia is strongly committed to prevent and eliminate this problem from the world of work as well as from society as a whole.
In fact, the Royal Government has set a number of policies and implemented both national and international standards, as well as cooperation with development partners, especially with organizations, which in recent years have been actively involved with the Ministry of Labor in promoting occupational safety and health. Of women workers and co-founded the principles of prevention and control of all kinds of violence and harassment in the workplace.
Mr. Ith Sam Heng expressed his hope that the whole workshop will actively participate in discussions to better understand the issues ahead and to work together in good faith to achieve common goals to protect workers, especially women. Violence and harassment in the workplace.
In order for this workshop to run smoothly and effectively, the Minister made the following recommendations: 1. Violence and all kinds of harassment in the workplace are diseases that need to be treated. 2: All parties must participate. To prevent and eliminate all forms of violence and harassment in the workplace.3 Unions Civil society organizations are an indispensable partner in achieving the Sustainable Development Goals.
At the same time, the Ministry of Labor also cooperates with CARE on some jobs in Cambodia, including: * Dissemination of Prakas 194 on working conditions, hygiene and occupational safety rules of enterprises, establishments, companies, entertainment services. * Training for entertainment establishments, shop owners. Working women. * Prepare teaching materials related to harassment. * Participate in creating policies on harassment in the workplace.
