(Phnom Penh): The Union of Federation of Cambodian Journalists (UCJJ) on October 16, 2019 issued a statement calling on the Stung Treng Provincial Court to prioritize the “Media Regime” Law in its lawsuit. Boulam MP on PNN reporter involved in land dispute in Ratanakkiri province.
The Union of Federation of Cambodian Journalists claims that it has researched and reviewed the content of PNN broadcasts on land disputes in Ratanakkiri province, with pictures of actual land locations and interviews with sources.
The KUF claimed that Boulam had previously taken PNN staff to the Ratanakkiri Provincial Court, but the Ratanakkiri Provincial Court had refused to take action. As for the Union of Cambodian Journalists, they received a report that after the broadcast of PNN TV, about 100 hectares of disputed land was heard by the authorities and all measures were taken to distribute it to the people.
The Union of Federation of Cambodian Journalists monitors PNN staff member Lon Vanna in front of the Stung Treng Provincial Court on the morning of October 16, 2019. Based on the above facts and stories, the Union of Cambodian Journalists believes:
First, PNN TV broadcasted about the land dispute in Ratanakkiri province in accordance with the media regime, with the TV station reaching the scene, citing sources and conducting interviews. The district governor was quoted as saying that he was involved in a land dispute and that he was involved with the Boulam MPs. That land dispute is already over.
Third, the Stung Treng Provincial Court action on the PNN TV case with Mr. Bou Lam's side is a legal procedure. However, the Union of Federation of Cambodian Journalists urges the court to consider using this law on the "media regime".
The Union of Federation of Cambodian Journalists hopes that the parties will show a spirit of tolerance, reconciliation and reconciliation, and the court will leave the case untouched.
