The meeting aims to exchange views on the policy priorities of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and examine opportunities for expanding private sector investment in agricultural production and initiatives related to the expansion of sustainable economic development; Sustainability in Cambodia. In addition, the ASEAN-US Business Council intends to exchange views on agricultural trade policy in Cambodia.
The Minister expressed his pleasure and welcome to the private sector investment projects in Cambodia, especially in the agricultural sector and some supporting infrastructure that supports the process of development of the agricultural value chain.
He also hoped that through cooperation with the ASEAN-US Business Council, Cambodia would be able to acquire new technologies and technologies in agricultural production and would be able to help improve the livelihoods of rural farmers.
He also hoped that the integration of technology in agriculture would also help improve the quality and safety of food, and that Cambodians were now paying close attention to quality and certified food to ensure quality. To well-being.
The Minister also requested the ASEAN-US Business Council to consider the possibility of assisting Cambodia in preventing African swine fever to support the pig industry in Cambodia.
Representatives of the ASEAN-US Business Council see the potential for investment in Cambodia, especially the introduction of new agricultural technologies that help reduce agricultural costs, especially to facilitate agricultural labor and reduce the risk of pollution. And reduce the use of agricultural inputs (fertilizers and chemicals).
For animal husbandry, the Business Council is interested in bringing to Cambodia some products that help reduce the environmental impact of ammonia in manure, introducing products that support biosecurity on the farm site. Raised to prevent infectious diseases such as African plague and a variety of products mixed with animal feed that can help immunize the disease and reduce the use of vaccines.
