Senior official of the Ministry of Women's Affairs: More and more women in senior positions in government institutions
(Phnom Penh): Ms. Nhean Sochetra, Director General of the Ministry of Women's Affairs, stated that the number of women working in the public sector has increased significantly and women have also held senior positions in the Royal Government. .
Ms. Nhean Sochetra claimed that from 1994 until now, the number of women involved in decision-making has increased significantly, with only seven women participating in the 1993 legislature. Out of 128 seats, but if you look at the current election in 2018, there are 25 women out of 125 seats, indicating that women make up about 20% of the legislature.
Ms. Nhean Sochetra made this remark while attending a press conference on the progress of the International Conference on Population and Development in Cambodia with the participation of Mr. Chea Chantum, Secretary General of General Secretariat of Population and Development, Ministry of Planning, Professor Tung Rathavy, Director of the National Center for Maternal and Child Health, Ministry of Health, and many journalists.
Ms. Nhean Sochetra said that for the Senate in 1999, there were 9 out of 61 women, equal to 14.75%, and in the 2017 election, there were 12 out of 62 women equal to 19. .35%. In particular, in the executive branch, compared to 1993 to 2018, there are three women ministers, including the Minister of National Assembly-Senate Relations and Inspection, the Minister of Women's Affairs and the Minister of Culture.
She stated that if there were 45 women in the position of Secretary of State, equal to 15.83%, and 69 Undersecretaries of State, equal to 65.33%, in the judiciary, the number of women also increased.
She added that the women in the sub-national position, there is a female governor, holding the governor of Koh Kong in 2018, which we did not have before.
Ms. Soeun Socheata, a woman representative of the Ministry of Education, said that the Ministry of Education is committed to ensuring gender equality, ensuring reproductive health, focusing on women and girls.
Currently, she said, kindergarten teachers are also male, but are at a low level, with 50% of primary school teachers, slightly more female teachers than male teachers, but more than 40% for lower secondary teachers, for upper secondary teachers. More than 30% of these are the growth and resilience of women in education.
