(Phnom Penh): The issue of the loss of Koh Tral is still a controversial issue in the Khmer view. The Cambodian opposition to the current Cambodian government has always tried to accuse the loss of Koh Tral to Vietnam because the government signed the 1985 treaty.
But the Cambodian government has repeatedly claimed that the 1985 border treaty with Vietnam was in accordance with the law between the two countries and in accordance with international law. In other words, this treaty is only a facilitation to demarcate and demarcate the border between the two countries, not a treaty to cut off land for Vietnam as alleged.
Wanting to end disputes and disputes over this territory, Samdech Techo Hun Sen during the National Assembly session on August 9, 2012 spent many hours explaining and clarifying the loss of territory, demarcation and demarcation. Post the border with Vietnam.
In that, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also raised the issue of the loss of Koh Tral. According to Samdech Techo Hun Sen, there are three major events that clearly testify that Koh Tral has been hidden in the hands of Vietnam for a long time.
Those events include: In 1939, French Governor Previer cut Koh Tral under Cochinchina rule. In 1949, the territory of Cochinchina was ceded to France by King Bao Dai and III. In 1964, His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk deposited the 1939 Previer Line at the United Nations, requesting international recognition and recognition of the maritime border between Cambodia and Vietnam.
