(Svay Rieng): Love is a life bond, a heartfelt love between two people. This heartfelt love is often seen in middle-aged people, but sometimes this love is not age-appropriate. In fact, a couple, a 48-year-old man, fell in love with a 72-year-old woman and agreed to take her to a wedding, much to the surprise of everyone in the village.
The old love affair led to this surprise in Svay Rieng province, and the ceremony to confirm the testimony of love took place this morning, October 13, 2019, at the woman's house in Tuol Angkob village, Sambour commune, Svay Teap district, Svay Rieng province. Svay Rieng.
The groom is 48-year-old Ung Vuntha, a resident of PRASAC village, PRASAC commune, Svay Chrum district, Svay Rieng province. The bride, 72-year-old Keut Lat, lives in Tuol Angkop village, Sambour commune, Svay Teap district, Svay Rieng province.
In an interview with Fresh News in Svay Rieng province, the groom said that he fell in love with his older bride because he saw her as a widow with two children before Pchum Ben. A month ago, I decided to get married, regardless of whether the bride is rich or poor. He continued that if you look at the woman's life, she also does not have any wealth, she loves her heart and mind.
Judging by the groom's gestures and descriptions, one can see that he is really loyal to the bride. The groom, Ung Vatta, even swore to live and sleep with the bride forever.
(Kratie): Senior Minister Kun Kim, First Vice Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management, encouraged people affected by the floods to replant their crops without despair. The senior minister also claimed that the Royal Government is always ready to help all the needs of the people in this cultivation.
Senior Minister Kun Kim's encouragement was made during the distribution of 175 tons of rice seeds to more than 2,000 families from three districts in Kratie province this morning, October 13, 2019.
According to the report of the National Committee for Disaster Management, the floods during the last rainy season affected more than 2,000 hectares of rice fields in Kratie province, equivalent to 2,296 families. During the rice seed distribution ceremony this morning, Senior Minister Kun Kim expressed regret for the damage, but also encouraged people to replant to get rice to eat. Especially to be planted when it rains.
"It is very unfortunate for the damage to your crops. This is a natural disaster that we can hardly avoid, even if we do not want to. However, now that the water has receded, I urge you to try to replant, if we do not have much to sell, we can get some back for food. This is the confirmation of the Senior Minister.
On the occasion of distributing rice seeds to the people this morning, Senior Minister Kun Kim also brought a message from Samdech Techo Hun Sen to visit the victims.
Mr. Kun Kim also informed the people that Samdech Techo always thinks about the well-being and hardships of the people affected by the floods. Samdech Techo also constantly reminded the national and sub-national authorities to solve all the problems of the people. Ministry of Environment uses a chainsaw to crack down on crime to make a giant elephant to preserve biodiversity
(Mondulkiri): A statue of an elephant built using a chainsaw as evidence collected from natural resource crimes has been completed and is scheduled to be displayed to the public at Sen Monorom Park in Mondulkiri province in Coming soon.
He said that the elephant statues were built in order to show the public the chainsaw exhibits that were confiscated and collected and stored at the five wildlife sanctuaries in the area. Mondulkiri Province.
In addition, it also wants to show that chainsaws are a means of committing the crime of encroaching on the state's forest land and in order to stop the use of chainsaws, which leads to the destruction of natural resources and turn to an iron elephant. Made from chainsaw and want to awaken the people to participate in activities to protect and conserve live elephants in order to increase income from tourism in Mondulkiri.
Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment, Mr. Neth Pheaktra, pointed out that the Mondulkiri Provincial Department of Environment has set up a 7-month installation of a statue of an elephant with a chainsaw in Mondulkiri province with the financial support of Donors and development partners to participate in the design and installation of elephant statues made from this chainsaw.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment said that on March 25, 2017, a herd of 12 elephants fell into a pit of water and was trapped in the mud, causing a surprise in O'Am village, Sre Khtom commune, Keo Seima district, Mondulkiri province.
A team from the Mondulkiri Provincial Department of Environment, in collaboration with WCS, and the villagers dug a road that would allow the herd to emerge from the mud and return safely to the forest. Another event was the herd of elephants, which often appeared behind the Keo Seima District Hall administration building, and their appearances near people's homes and crops in Keo Seima district, which caused a conflict between elephants and humans. Original.
According to Mr. Net Pheaktra, Cambodia currently has about 500 wild elephants living in Cambodia, according to a study of wild elephants in the five wildlife sanctuaries in Mondulkiri province. In total, there are more than 375 elephants, in the Cardamom Mountains, there are about 200 elephants and others are living in protected areas of Cambodia.
The system of protected areas and biodiversity corridors covers an area of 7.5 million hectares, equivalent to 41% of the total area of Cambodia that is being protected and conserved by the Ministry of Environment.
According to a study by the Ministry of Environment, the number of wild elephants in Cambodia, as well as in other countries in Southeast Asia, has declined sharply in recent decades due to poaching and habitat destruction. Elephants and conflicts between elephants and humans, while some people's fields are planted next to protected areas, and sometimes elephants come to eat crops.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra pointed out that the number of domestic elephants raised by the locals is about 70. The number of domestic elephants is actually declining due to lack of natural reproduction, and some elephants are aging and dying.
