"Syria should be liberated from foreign troops and Russia will leave if Damascus no longer needs us," said the Russian president.
MOSCOW (Reuters) - Russian President Vladimir Putin has said that Syria's territorial integrity should be fully restored and that all foreign troops, including Russia, should withdraw if Damascus no longer needs Moscow's help. This is according to the release by RT on Sunday, October 13, 2019.
Putin made the remarks in an interview with RT in the Arabian Parliament on Friday. "All foreign troops illegally deployed in Syria must leave ... and, of course, if the Syrian government says they will not leave," he said. "We need the help of the Russian military. We will withdraw from Syria as well."
Putin's remarks come as the Turkish military is invading northern Syria to crush Kurdish YPG militants, which Ankara considers terrorists, and create a safe haven for civilians. Fleeing Syria. But many European countries have condemned and called on Turkey to immediately suspend military operations, while France has announced a halt to the sale of military equipment to Turkey.
