Doctors at Singapore Medical Center: High blood pressure is a hidden killer, pregnant women may have the highest risk of developing high blood pressure
(Phnom Penh): High blood pressure does not have any specific symptoms, so most diagnoses are found when patients come for a general health examination. Initial blood pressure measurements do not determine the diagnosis because sometimes the patient may have high blood pressure. This is what Dr. Yin Sithika, a doctor in charge of gynecology at the Singapore Medical Center, said in a press conference.
Dr. Yin Sithika also said that in general, the majority of patients with high blood pressure in the early stages do not show any specific symptoms, which can be called a "hidden killer." However, some patients may experience symptoms such as headache, anxiety, nausea, ringing in the ears, sweating and frequent nosebleeds, which can make patients feel that they have high blood pressure.
"Pregnant women with a history of high blood pressure should see their doctor at least once every two weeks to keep their blood pressure at 130 to 80," she said. "The aim is to make the mother-to-be feeding of the baby work well."
Because high blood pressure does not have any specific symptoms, most diagnoses are made when a patient comes for a general checkup, she said. An appropriate diagnosis is made by measuring the patient's blood pressure at least twice a week or by having the patient monitor his or her blood pressure at home using a sphygmomanometer.
According to Mr. Mean Veasna, General Practitioner of General Practitioner and Pediatrician of Singapore Medical Center, to date, high blood pressure has not been cured. However, drug therapy can bring blood pressure back to normal, avoiding the complications and risks of high blood pressure.
Please note that future complications due to this disease can kill people unknowingly, so if not treated properly, it can manifest as some major complications such as heart attack, heart failure, stroke or stroke. Prolonged high blood pressure can lead to atherosclerosis, kidney failure and eye damage.
