On that occasion, Mr. Mikami Masahiro highly appreciated and commended the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia for its excellent work and the Cambodia-UN Joint Court not only contributing to bring justice to the victims of The Khmer Rouge regime, however, contributed to justice and the peace process for the whole world.
"Japan continues to support the operation of the tribunal and seeks to provide more financial support," he said.
Mr. Mikami Masahiro also met with the management of the Office of Administration of the Khmer Rouge Tribunal after a visit to the courtroom and a presentation by the Director of Public Affairs.
"Japan's financial contribution to support the operation of the Khmer Rouge tribunal, bringing former senior leaders of the Khmer Rouge regime and those responsible for serious crimes to trial, is indeed available," said Tony Kranh. "It is important to be involved in the process of bringing justice to the victims, as well as to be involved in national reconciliation and peace in Cambodia."
At the same time, Mr. Kranh Tony informed the Ambassador of Japan about the new developments of the Khmer Rouge Tribunal in terms of case processing, finances, and dissemination of court proceedings to the public. Outreach work, which so far the Khmer Rouge tribunal has been very successful in, both inside and outside the courts, has benefited nearly 700,000 members of the public.
It should be noted that the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia (ECCC) was established in early February 2006 and until 2019, there have been four cases, of which Case 001 against the former Director of Tuol Sleng Prison has been closed. Kaing Guek Eav alias Duch was sentenced to life in prison.
Five pilgrimages to the Mekong region to promote Buddhism for the second time in mid-October
(Phnom Penh): The second five-region pilgrimage to spread Buddhism in the Mekong region, including Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia and Vietnam, will be held from October 14 to 31, 2019. .
The parade in these five countries is organized by a convoy of cars and a procession of monks from Chiang Rai, Thailand on October 14 to Myanmar, Laos, Vietnam and back in Siem Reap on October 31. The year 2019 is expected to be attended by a large number of people, as the five countries have also agreed to allow parades without passports to be used for joint missions.
The second Five-Territorial Pilgrimage continues to receive the same condolences from the Royal Highness of Cambodia, as in the past, by Royal Highness Tep Rong and Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of National Defense.
It should be reminded that the view of the first 5 land pilgrimage on October 27, 2017 in Kampot province of the Kingdom of Cambodia, there are many things that can not be forgotten. Raindrops are like magic water falling from the sky and can not overcome the force of pure transparency for the Buddhists in Cambodia who have Buddhism.
While a group of pilgrims were in a bus moving along National Road 3 through Kampot province towards Vietnam, they suddenly encountered Cambodian Buddhists standing in the rain waiting for the procession to pass by. Clear to place bowls and drinks and suffixes for the monks of the five countries is the most beautiful picture.
Due to the strength of this generosity, the five monks of the territory and the group of devotees had to stop their cars to take the Ben Bath from the Cambodian people. Due to time constraints, the monks could not be summoned anywhere due to the arrangements to receive the group of pilgrims from 5 lands.
The journey in this procession was not favorable at that time, the sky was black and rainy, but the group traveled for a week and the pilgrims considered this to be a blessing from the angels. Due to the fact that no matter how much it rains, when the monks go to Ben Bath or planting Po trees, the falling rain must stop and disappear immediately.
Man can also be born as an angel if the mind before death thinks of the good deeds left in this world, which are like the eight great virtues of giving, listening to sermons, building something for the public good. And fear of sin.
In Koh Kong province of the Kingdom of Cambodia, it is another point where the five groups of pilgrims together believe that the deities have blessed the journey to Wat Samuth Theram, located on the seashore. The amazing thing is that just as the pilgrims arrive, the drops, which are thickened, turn into small, flower-like grains.
