(Siem Reap): APSARA Authority, Embassy of India in Phnom Penh and Vichetra India Export Co., Ltd. will jointly organize the 1st "Cardi - Indian Handicraft" Exhibition at the Museum of Asian Traditional Textiles in Siem Reap-Angkor on 12-14 October 2019 to celebrate the 150th anniversary of Mahatma Gandhi under the chairmanship of Senior Minister Cham Prasidh, Minister of Industry and Handicrafts.
During his lifetime, the Indian hero left a cultural legacy in which Indians turned to the production and use of self-made fabrics in order to maintain the national identity and promote the national economy by the Indians themselves. .
She said that Mahatma Gandhi's main view was that in India there are many classes, many religions, many people, but by wearing the same clothes made by Indians will make Indians look equal. Collaborate, and no matter where you go, no matter what your religion, no matter what your class, you still look like an Indian.
Therefore, Indian-made fabrics were an important part of uniting the Indian people to preserve their traditional occupations, the raw materials for the production of fabrics, the building of the national economy and the exploitation of the British colonies, especially the populace. Indians stopped valuing foreign (British) products and turning to local products and maintaining their national identity reflecting themselves by wearing this garment.
