A statue of the head of Lokesvara 1 was found while excavating to repair the east gate of Ta Nei temple
(Siem Reap): A sandstone bodhisattva's head was found while experts from the Apsara Authority were excavating at the east entrance of Ta Temple in the southwest corner.
According to the APSARA Authority, this excavation is to find the roof stone that fell buried in the pile of stones and soil to repair the temple on the morning of October 8, 2019.
According to Mr. Sea Sophearun, Project Manager of Ta Nei Temple Restoration Project and Archaeologist of the Department of Temple Preservation in Angkor Park and Preventive Archeology, There is only the head, there is the image of the Buddha on the hair, there is the right ear, but the left nostril, the nose, the face, one side of the stone peel.
It is unknown at this time what caused the damage, but it could have been a collision, rupture or poor quality of the stone on one cheek, causing the stone to peel off.
On the other hand, this sculpture may not have been in this location in the beginning because there is no evidence that there is a body in this place as well.
Mr. Chhouk Somala, Officer in Charge of Archaeological Inventory of the Department of Temple Preservation in Angkor Park and Preventive Archeology of the APSARA Authority, inspected and measured the size of the head of the Bodhisattva Lokesvara, said that the head is made of tall sandstone. 54 cm wide, 27 cm wide and 36 cm thick.
The bodhisattva's head, or lord, is currently being held at the APSARA Authority for temporary registration and photographing, as well as the preparation for the Angkor Preservation.
