US Senate is preparing a "huge package of sanctions" to drop on Turkey!
WASHINGTON: The US Senate has drafted a bill to punish Turkey for launching a military operation in northern Syria, possibly leading to an arms embargo on the alliance. This is according to a quote from Sputnik newspaper on the morning of Thursday, October 10, 2019.
These include restrictions on visa bans by Turkish leaders, sanctions on other countries cooperating with Turkey in the energy and military fields, and sanctions on Turkey over the purchase of the S-400 Russian missile system. And a ban on US arms sales to Turkey. Donald Trump also expressed support for sanctions against Turkey over the launch of Turkish military operations in northern Syria.
In Syria, the United States has supported Kurdish fighters in the fight against ISIS, but Kurdish forces are seen by Turkey as a pawn of the PKK in Turkey, which Ankara considers a terrorist organization. More than 70 people in Turkey have also been arrested for spreading propaganda about Turkish military operations in Syria.
On Wednesday, Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan announced the start of a military operation in northern Syria against ISIS and Kurds. Erdogan said the goal of the operation was to eliminate any threats and establish a safe zone in the area. The United States has withdrawn its troops from northern Syria and has said it does not support or warn against Turkish military operations.
