(Phnom Penh): General Ith Sarath, Deputy Commander-in-Chief and Joint Chief of Staff of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, said that Cambodia and Pakistan will work to promote closer and better relations and cooperation between the two armies. More.
During the meeting, General Ith Sarath, on behalf of the leadership of the Ministry of National Defense, the Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, extended a warm welcome to the presence of Lt. Gen. Mohammad Jirak Heider and members of the delegation on the territory. Cambodia, he considered very important for the strengthening of cooperation, fruitful relations and closer ties between the armies of the two countries, Cambodia and Pakistan to improve.
General Ith Sarath said that both in the region and globally are facing many problems, especially terrorism. Cambodia does not currently exist, but Cambodia already has an anti-terrorism unit ready to fight it, because we do not know when and where this terrorism took place.
Regarding the recruitment of Royal Cambodian Armed Forces (RCAF) trainees for training in Pakistan and the strengthening of cooperation between the two armies, the General requested that the Joint Chiefs of Staff of the Pakistan Army Command consider further admission to the trainees of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces. The Royal Cambodian Armed Forces (RCAF) also went to Pakistan for training.
General Ith Sarath continued that Cambodia, under the wise leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, has led the country to peace and stability and rapid progress in all areas.
Lt. Gen. Mohammad Jirak Heider confirmed that he would accept any request from the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces and would report to the Pakistani Ministry of Defense on Cambodia's request.
At the same time, Lt. Gen. Mohammad Jirak Heider also invited General Ith Sarath to pay an official and friendly visit to Pakistan at a reasonable time for our two countries to strengthen ties. Cooperation in strengthening ties of solidarity and friendship through the exchange of delegations to study, experiment and explore the mutual progress of the two armies.
