ASEAN environment ministers plant cardamom trees in front of Angkor Wat to show love for green environment and biodiversity
(Siem Reap): A delegation of environment ministers from ASEAN member countries, including delegations from China, Japan and South Korea, jointly planted cardamom and thorn trees in front of Angkor Wat to show their love for the green environment and create There is all kinds of biodiversity in the area, which is a great heritage of the motherland of Cambodia.
The planting of cardamom and thnong tree took place on October 08, 2019 in the presence of Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Economy and Finance, Representative of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia after the opening ceremony. The 15th ASEAN Environment Ministers and Related Meetings 2019.
550 cardamom trees representing Siem Reap province and 100 thnong trees were planted on 1 hectare.
According to Mr. Neth Pheak, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, the purpose of planting 550 cardamom trees and 100 thnong trees is to increase biodiversity to protect the environment and ecosystem.
The Cardamom Planting Program in Angkor, Siem Reap Province is an initiative of Mr. Say Sam Al, Minister of the Environment, in order to recall and show the plants or flowers in the geographical location or province as in the meaning of the original sound of the Golden Voice emperor. Sin Sisamouth performed through the songs Kravan Siem Reap, Romduol Kratie and Champa Battambang.
The 15th Meeting of the ASEAN Environment Ministers and Related Meetings 2019, hosted by Cambodia from 7-10 October 2019 in the territory of Siem Reap Angkor, with the participation of the ASEAN Environment Ministers And a delegation of 10 ASEAN Secretary-Generals, Environment Ministers from China, Japan and South Korea.
ASEAN Youth Association expands programs to strengthen closer ties between people and people in the region
(Siem Reap): Last week, 39 young ASEAN community leaders gathered in Singapore to exchange views as well as discuss issues and opportunities for cooperation for a sustainable partnership in the Association Program. The second ASEAN Youth (AYF) organized by the Singapore International Organization (SIF) and the National Youth Council (NYC).
Three young Vietnamese leaders also attended the event and shared their experiences in both social and economic fields. Under the theme "Partnership for Sustainable ASEAN Community", this year's program focuses on the importance of warm people-to-people contacts to ensure the social, environmental and economic sustainability of the region.
Following the success of its first year in 2018, the Singapore-based ASEAN Youth Association program has also expanded its overseas presence to Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia this year to increase exchanges. And other opportunities for this cooperation.
"The ASEAN Youth Association program demonstrates the value of multicultural friendship in a rapidly changing global context," said Jean Tan, Executive Director of Singapore International. We see the emergence of many young ASEAN leaders as potential pioneers to help
By promoting mutual understanding and enabling staff efforts to create a positive and sustainable impact. Singapore International is very proud to be a part of this meaningful program and proud to contribute to our youth's efforts to build a cohesive ASEAN community. More cohesiveness and sustainability.
Such remarks by Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth took place on October 8, 2019, on the occasion of his invitation to represent the Prime Minister of Cambodia Samdech Techo Hun Sen at the official opening ceremony of the meeting. Fifth, the ASEAN Environment Ministers and related meetings 2019 hosted by Cambodia in Siem Reap.
Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth suggested that "coordination and management, as well as increase resources to deal with wildfires and cross-border smog." "ASEAN must ensure land use planning, sustainable forest management and fresh air in the region."
The Deputy Prime Minister said that Cambodia is very pleased with the establishment of the ASEAN Coordinating Center for Transboundary Smog (ACC-THPC), which will be located in Indonesia.
"We need to continue to strengthen our cooperation, especially on prevention mechanisms such as law enforcement and enforcement, stopping forest fires and controlling fires to turn ASEAN into a smoke-free zone," he said. "In addition, we need to ensure an effective and timely response to these fires when the ASEAN Agreement on Transboundary Haze Pollution enters into force."
