The draft law on consumer protection has been approved by the National Assembly (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): The draft law on consumer protection, which has 11 chapters and 51 articles, has been fully approved by the National Assembly with 105 votes in favor. The plenary session of the National Assembly on the afternoon of October 28, 2019.
During the discussion of the draft law, Ms. Nin Saphon, Chairperson of the Public Works, Transport, Telecommunication, Post, Industry, Mining, Energy, Trade, Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction Committee of the National Assembly, reported to the National Assembly on the results of the study of the draft law. Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce and his associates, representing the Royal Government, defended the draft law.
The draft law will be a law to protect the interests of consumers from the actions and unfair practices of traders in business activities, protect the rights and interests of consumers, support the market to be competitive in terms of price, quality of goods and services.
The draft Consumer Protection Law was first drafted by the Royal Government, headed by the Ministry of Commerce, on May 27, 2012 with the support of the Asian Development Bank (ADB). This draft law was discussed in detail in technical level meetings and inter-ministerial meetings with the participation of all relevant ministries and institutions of the Royal Government with a high sense of responsibility.
This draft law is set out in the work program and action plan of the Ministry of Commerce, which is included in the policy of the Royal Government. Draft consumer protection laws are an important part of the global legal system that promotes effective market economy.
This draft law is designed not only to protect consumers from the actions and misconduct of traders, but also to provide information to consumers and allow relevant regulators to establish regulations on information standards that traders must comply with. Follow. This draft law complements the relevant laws and regulations on consumer protection, including the Civil Code.
The draft law on consumer protection was approved by the Cabinet at the plenary session of the Council of Ministers on July 12, 2019.
