This is the statement made by Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment on the morning of October 6, 2019 at the closing ceremony of the EU Green Week Campaign with the organization of environmental sanitation activities in public places in Chroy Changvar district, Phnom Penh. .
"The European Union encourages reform, but reform alone is not enough," he said. "On behalf of the EU Delegation to Cambodia and our Member States, I call on all to say no to disposable plastic products."
According to a report by the Ministry of Environment, the amount of urban waste that Cambodia generates per day is more than 10,000 tons, which people in the capital and major cities are estimated to generate 1 kg of garbage per day. People living in rural areas generate about half a kilo of garbage a day. In total, the amount of waste that Cambodia generates each year is about 4 million tons, of which 20% of this waste is plastic bag waste.
The major provinces that produce large amounts of waste include: Phnom Penh, about 3,000 tons per day, Sihanoukville, about 700 tons per day, and Siem Reap, about 400 tons per day. It is estimated that the percentage of plastic collected through street cleaning in three cities is 17.3% in Phnom Penh, 20.7% in Siem Reap and 34% in Sihanoukville.
At the same time, the Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment mentioned the impact of plastic bags, saying that the reason why thin plastic bags are considered to be polluting the environment is that it takes hundreds of years to decompose, meaning that plastic waste Is a substance that does not decompose or decompose easily.
The sub-decree also stipulates additional charges for the use of plastic bags by customers. Restrictions on the use of plastic bags are not limited to Cambodia, there are many countries that have been implementing them.
Business owners and service owners who provide plastic bags to customers who volunteer to participate in programs to reduce the use of plastic bags will also be encouraged. People need to know that using too many plastic bags is like throwing plastic bags on the streets and in rivers. Cut down on plastic bags by putting the goods in one bag.
"Plastic is not good for our health, for the environment and for tourism," he said. When plastic bags dissolve chemicals, some substances affect human health and the environment. "When plastic is buried in the ground for many years, it will gradually dissolve and some chemicals will be released, affecting the lives of people and animals."
The Secretary of State added that in order to reduce the use of plastic and throw it away, people need to change their habits. Together, reduce plastic because plastic waste is a major threat to ecosystems and human development worldwide and in Cambodia. Participants in the public space cleaning program collected tons of garbage and plastic waste.
Mr. Net Pheaktra emphasized: "Walking to pick up trash now is a symbol of cleaning up the environment and we want the environment around us to be clean forever, not just now. "Therefore, there is a need for participation from all walks of life, and those who have a habit of littering, please change your attitude and participate in cleaning our house, cleaning our community with high responsibility."
