Understand the law: the crime of illegal drug trafficking
(Phnom Penh): The presence of drugs is a big part of causing concern to the people and society, as well as seriously affecting public order.
A small number of selfish people take the opportunity to illegally sell and trade drugs without caring about the negative effects on the nation.
To control and prevent the illegal trafficking of drugs, Cambodia has established the "Law on Drug Control" for the purpose of monitoring, preventing and combating drugs.
To know more about what kind of activities are considered drug trafficking? And to what extent should the perpetrators of this crime be punished?
Therefore, for this week, Fresh News' Legal Awareness Program, in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation, would like to raise the topic of "Illegal Drug Trafficking" to the following students: :
According to Article 40 of the Law on Drug Control 2012, "Illegal drug trafficking" is the provision for the sale, purchase, sale or exchange and illegal exchange of drugs.
Table 1 refers to plants and substances that cause serious harm and have no medical benefits, including heroin, marijuana, marijuana resin, marijuana oil. Table 2: Plants and substances that cause serious harm but have medical benefits, including cocaine, opium, water, opium poppy, morphine, methamphetamine.
For aggravating circumstances related to the above offenses, the sentence is set as follows:
For the possession, concealment of inventory and other similar activities and all forms of transmission that lead to the movement from one place to another, from one person to another, from one country to another, or through Sending traffic, importing and exporting drugs is also punishable under Article 40 of the Law on Drug Control.
In short, the law on drug control carries a strict sentence of life imprisonment. At the same time, the Royal Government of Cambodia has been actively campaigning to combat illegal drugs in order to eradicate drug trafficking and use throughout the country.
New Generation Sisowath Quay School Receives Volunteer Donations from Students' Parents More Than Expected
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Som Komsan, Deputy Principal of Preah Sisowath High School in charge of the new generation school, claimed that the reform of the education system through the new generation school was very supportive because the school actually received voluntary contributions from the parents of Students exceed expectations.
Mr. Samy Komsan made the remarks on the occasion of the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports held a celebration of Teachers' Day on October 5, 2019 at the National Institute of Education.
Mr. Sam Komsan said that the reform of the Ministry of Education through the new generation of schools at Preah Sisowath High School has been very successful. It has been many years since a new generation of schools was launched, and this year 2019 is the first year that schools require donations. Reform through the new generation of schools requires a lot of investment, and the principle of paying contributions from parents of students is made only to maintain the sustainability of investment.
According to Sam Komsan, the new generation of students is more than 900, and the school board has met to set a $ 280 contribution fee. However, the parents of some of the students volunteered to contribute $ 500 and $ 800, thus exceeding expectations.
The principal of Preah Sisowath High School stated: “Generally, we calculate $ 300 per year, so this investment is huge by our country's standards, but not much compared to other countries. Therefore, long-term, sustainable investments must involve guardians and communities through voluntary contributions. "This is the first year that we have received donations. In just two weeks, we have received a total of $ 210,000."
The new generation of Preah Sisowath High School, the first public school to use modern technology in line with 21st century school policy. New Generation School is a new curriculum that the Ministry of Education has implemented in the pilot phase in 2015. This practice is also done in both primary and secondary schools in order to improve education in Cambodia as well. And some countries in the region and the world.
There are currently 10 schools and are implementing this new generation school curriculum. There are 4 primary schools and 6 secondary schools.
These schools include: Preah Sisowath High School, Hun Sen High School, Kampong Cham High School, Prek Leap High School, Samdech Chea Sim High School, Prek Anchanh High School, Hun Sen Peam Chi Kang High School, Kork Pring Secondary School, Hun Sen Primary School, Kampong Cham Primary School, Preah Reach Akka Misey Primary School, Angkor Ban Primary School and Prohot Primary School.
The curriculum focuses on integrating technology into the education of both learners and educators, as well as providing a wide range of learning tools. In particular, the study time is longer than the old curriculum.
Preah Sisowath High School Deputy Principal said, "New Generation School is a new program of public schools after the previous reforms, which makes the education program more modern and similar to schools in developed countries and Developing. In particular, to improve the quality of education and to meet the needs of the labor market.
