(Phnom Penh): Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, on October 3, 2019, emphasized that in the nine months from the beginning of the year to the end of September 2019, the Government of Japan has provided a total of nearly $ 60 million to Cambodia.
Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon stated that from the beginning of the year to the end of September, the Royal Government of Cambodia and the Government of Japan signed the following three letters of exchange and minutes of discussions on Japanese financing:
First, on February 20, 2019, signed a document on Japanese financing totaling 4,939 million yen (approximately US $ 43 million), including: Concessional Loans for Irrigation Improvement and Rehabilitation Project; West Tonle Sap Water Release System Phase 2 and Construction Loan Financing for the Implementation of the Port Data Transmission System Development Project to Modernize the Port.
Second: On May 31, 2019, in Tokyo, Japan, under the chairmanship of Samdech Techo Hun Sen and the Prime Minister of Japan, signed a document on the provision of a total grant of 539 million yen (approximately 5 million US dollars). Implement two projects: Human Resource Development Scholarship Project and Warehouse Construction Project in Sihanoukville Autonomous Port Special Economic Zone.
Third: On July 17, 2019, signed a document on non-construction financing of 500 million yen (approximately 4.6 million US dollars) for the supply of road repair machinery in Cambodia.
Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon added that the Japanese government has not only made a significant contribution to peace and stability in Cambodia, but has also been actively supporting socio-economic recovery and development.
Please be informed that the Government of Japan, through its Ambassador to Cambodia, on October 3 signed an exchange of letters on its grant financing totaling 2.777 billion yen (nearly 26 million yen). USD) for the implementation of the project "Development of sewage system in Phnom Penh".
Through this grant, Japan will build a wastewater treatment plant before discharging it into Choeung Eang Lake, south of Phnom Penh. This Japanese assistance will make a significant contribution to improving the living environment in Cambodia.
Chipmung Industries launches safety campaign under the theme "Safety starts with me"
The safety campaign was held on October 2, 2019 at the Chip Mong Concrete Factory in Sihanoukville with the participation and cooperation of the guest speaker, Mr. Nut Ly, Deputy Director of the Department of Public Works and Transport of Sihanoukville.
Mr. An Sideth, Chief Safety Officer of Chip Mong Industries, stated that the purpose of this safety campaign is to contribute to the strengthening and dissemination of culture to social safety, especially traffic safety in the workplace and on the road, namely to reduce accidents. All traffic.
He added that the company's management always pays attention to safety first. Accidents can happen if we are not careful, so the dissemination of knowledge about driving safety on the road traffic law is really important for all drivers to avoid accidents eventually.
"The participation of the private sector in road safety education is important for public order and traffic accident prevention," said Nuth Ly, deputy director of the Sihanoukville Provincial Department of Public Works and Transport. Cambodia persuades Myanmar to buy Kampot pepper
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia on October 3, 2019 requested the Government of Myanmar to consider importing Kampot pepper, one of the most famous agricultural products in the world. This was confirmed by Ms. Tout Panha, Undersecretary of State at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation of Cambodia.
"The Deputy Prime Minister mentioned the exchange of agricultural goods," she told reporters at the conference. In Cambodia, we have the world-famous Kampot pepper product, and he also asked the Myanmar side to consider importing Kampot pepper to Myanmar.
According to the Undersecretary of State at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, the new Ambassador of Myanmar has agreed to work to boost trade and agriculture, but has not confirmed that he will import Kampot pepper.
Ms. Panha emphasized that the trade and investment sector between Cambodia and Myanmar is growing but at a very low level. In 2017, the trade volume between the two countries was more than 4 million US dollars, and in 2019, it was only more than 10 million US dollars.
The new Ambassador of Myanmar arrived in Cambodia on August 10, 2019. He presented his credentials to His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni, King of Cambodia on August 21.
