(Phnom Penh): Professor Eng Huot, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health of Cambodia, said that the main risk factors that lead to "non-communicable diseases" caused by improper diet, tobacco use, alcohol and inactivity in exercise. Non-communicable diseases include diabetes, cardiovascular disease, chronic respiratory disease and cervical cancer.
Mr. Eng Huot's statement was made this morning, October 2, 2019, on the occasion of the participation of the Ministry of Health of Cambodia, the Russian Embassy in Cambodia, the World Health Organization in Cambodia, and the United Nations Development Program in Cambodia. Organize a workshop to disseminate reports on investment cases in non-communicable diseases in Cambodia at the Cambodiana Hotel in Phnom Penh.
On that occasion, a senior official of the Ministry of Health added that health risks caused by pollution, environmental health risks, urbanization in urban areas and climate change are becoming another threat to public health and put more burden on Risk factors for non-communicable diseases.
According to Professor Eng Huot, measures to prevent non-communicable diseases or reduce the risk of disease risk factors are started from individuals, from individual families, from individual communities to the whole society through health promotion education activities for the people. Citizens adopt a healthy lifestyle that does not face the risk factors of non-communicable diseases.
He said that the Ministry of Health has developed policies, strategic plans, national action plans, such as the multi-sectoral national plan for the prevention and management of non-communicable diseases and relevant national standards for the prevention and control of non-communicable diseases, which are priority diseases such as diabetes and heart disease. Vascular, chronic respiratory diseases and cancer, such as cervical cancer.
It should be noted that according to the report of the World Health Organization 2018, it is estimated that non-communicable diseases represent about 64% of all deaths in Cambodia.
