(Phnom Penh): Mr. Som Komsan, Deputy Principal of Preah Sisowath High School in charge of New Generation School, said that digital education does not require students to be illiterate, knowing or not depends on practice The more practice you write at home, the better the quality of writing will be.
"There is a lot of concern from some people about digital education, which will make it impossible for students to write or write incorrectly," he said. "Regarding this opinion, I do not have anything to deny, because usually more or less always, but you all have to rethink which advantages and disadvantages are more."
He added that digital learning does not require all students to throw away their pens and pencils. For writing or any other work is still the same because these things are done under the control of the brain.
"In the opinion that digital learning will require students to be illiterate, it is not a correct view, it is just a one-sided view, everything depends on the students' performance," he added.
In relation to the education sector, Mr. Sam Komsan also informed about some plans to develop the education sector in the new generation of schools, stating that the new generation of schools is not just planning something to do immediately. The school has a plan for the next 3 or 5 years.
He said that in the new generation of schools, students who graduate from high school, they will have enough ability to be able to continue their studies in various fields, they have the ability to research or other tasks without difficulty.
He also added that for other plans, the school will organize the curriculum and lessons of teachers to share to students digitally, which can be placed in the digital library so that students can study and research. In addition, the school will set up a question bank for students to learn and have the opportunity to test their knowledge so that students know where they are learning and fill in the gaps.
