Strike Awareness - Demonstration was introduced to the police as of August 2019, 35 strikes had taken place
(Central): Additional knowledge related to the settlement of labor disputes and the right to strike and demonstrate peacefully has been introduced to about 30 police officers who are key trainers in this field through an ongoing training course. On October 1-2, 2019 at the Police Academy of Cambodia.
The training was organized by the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training in collaboration with the Ministry of Interior, the National Police Commissioner, the Cambodian Police Academy, the International Labor Organization (ILO) and the Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights (OHCHR). .
This training course is organized for the purpose of:
Som Chamnan, Undersecretary of State at the Ministry of Labor, who presided over the event, said that the right to strike and demonstrate peacefully was guaranteed by the Cambodian Constitution. Article 37 of the Constitution states that "the right to strike and to demonstrate peacefully shall be exercised within the framework of the law."
In practice, we observe that strikes often occur after workers and employers are unable to find a solution to collective labor disputes that occur in their enterprises, and in some cases, some strikes turn into Illegal strikes or violent demonstrations that require the intervention of the competent authorities and judicial police officers.
The Undersecretary of State added that better working conditions and harmonization of industrial relations are the foundation of sustainable employment development. In this regard, the Ministry of Labor has reformed the labor inspection system, strengthened the implementation of the Labor Law and the Union Law, and improved working conditions and industrial relations.
In fact, through the implementation of the law on unions, the number of professional organizations registered with the Ministry of Labor totaled 5,117 as of August 2019, of which 4,873 local unions, 202 federations, 31 unions and 31 employers' associations 11 In addition, the resolution of labor disputes has been effective, with the number of labor disputes including a decrease while the number of strikes has also decreased.
It should be noted that the resolution of joint labor disputes by the Arbitration Council achieved a resolution of 70%. In 2018, there were 47 strikes, a decrease of 50 cases compared to 2017. In 2019, there were 35 strikes as of the end of August. National Assembly Holds Standing Committee Meeting to Discuss and Approve 7 Agenda
