Editorial: Smiles of Khmer Buddhists under the shadow of peace
(Phnom Penh): The 15-day Kan Ben and Pchum Ben festivals were closed on Saturday, September 28, 2019. Kan Ben and Pchum Ben are important festivals for Cambodians who have a strong belief in Buddhism.
Kan Ben or Pchum Ben, although there are many different reasons for this festival. But for now, this festival has become a precious time for the Cambodian people in gratitude for their grandparents, parents, elders, both the elderly and those who have passed away.
In order to give the Cambodian people enough time to celebrate or pay tribute to their loved ones in the annual holiday calendar, the Royal Government of Cambodia has set three days off during Of the celebration of the festival.
During the three-day holiday, Cambodians, especially those who have left the country to study and earn a living, both at home and abroad, have traveled to their hometowns. Of them. Phnom Penh, the town of people from all over, used to be bustling and crowded, with the number of people and vehicles also turning quiet.
A big smile is displayed on the faces of Cambodian Buddhists as they perform meritorious deeds for their ancestors with their families. This smile shows the happiness of the Cambodian people when they do good deeds for the rich.
The joyful gathering of meritorious deeds on Kan Ben and Pchum Ben, which is a precious Khmer tradition, depends on two main factors: the livelihood and the peace of the nation that is covering the whole country at this time. Currently.
In addition, along the national roads of Cambodia, they also observed the busy travel of people, both cars and motorcycles have increased so much that some roads are congested, which shows that Improving the living standards of the Cambodian people.
Livelihood is an important factor for people to be generous and go to the temple to celebrate Pchum Ben, but even more important is the shadow of peace.
